// A MODEL CITED FROM A DIFFERENT FILE THAN THE ONE THAT DECLARES IT.
//
// Before 2026-10-02 a model name had to be a bare local identifier: `model(I.M)` was a hard
// "unknown model". That made a model usable only inside its declaring file, so the only way to
// get its results elsewhere was for the declaring file to prove named theorems off it and for
// consumers to alias THOSE (which is what std/ring.b4m → std/integer/order.b4m does, and why
// the limitation went unnoticed).
//
// It is a real limitation though: a model is an INTERPRETATION of an abstract theory, and that
// interpretation is as reusable as any theorem proved from it. A consumer that wants a
// different corollary of the same abstract corpus had to either persuade the owning file to
// export it or re-declare the identical mapping.
//
// Now a qualified model name resolves the qualifier to an import and looks the model up in
// THAT file's namespace — the same two-step every qualified reference takes.
//
// This fixture is self-contained (one file, three sections) so it checks standalone; the
// cross-file path proper is exercised by `tests/cases/model_cited_across_files_user.b4m`.
sort Carrier
const UNIT: Carrier
func combine(a: Carrier, b: Carrier) => Carrier
axiom combineUnitLeft: forall a: Carrier; combine(UNIT, a) = a

theorem unitIsNeutral: forall a: Carrier; combine(UNIT, a) = a
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall a: Carrier; combine(UNIT, a) = a
    [by cite combineUnitLeft]
qed
